</nav></div></header><section class="article-hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="crumb"><a href="../thought-leadership">Thought Leadership</a> / Software Partners</div><div class="cat">Software Partners</div><h1>Why Device Management Becomes a Product Requirement for Software Partners</h1><p>Selling the first time clock is easy compared with operating a fleet across customers, resellers, sites and software versions. Device management becomes part of the partner product experience.</p><div class="meta"><span>ZKTeco WFM Insights</span><span>Educational Perspective</span><span>Updated September 2026</span></div></div></section><main class="content-wrap"><article class="article-body"><p class="lead">Selling the first time clock is easy compared with operating a fleet across customers, resellers, sites and software versions. Device management becomes part of the partner product experience. For HCM, WFM and T&amp;A software companies, the decision affects more than hardware. The endpoint becomes part of the partner’s own product experience, support model and long-term roadmap.</p><div class="use-case"><div class="kicker">REAL-WORLD SCENARIO</div><h2>One configuration error becomes 600 support calls.</h2><p>A partner has 600 clocks across dozens of customers. A new application setting is distributed manually by local administrators. Most sites update correctly, but a small group receives the wrong configuration and another group never receives the change because devices were offline.</p><p>Without centralized state, the support team cannot quickly answer which devices are on which version, which configuration was applied, or whether the issue is device, application or network related. At that point, device management is no longer an IT convenience—it is part of the product.</p></div><h2>Provisioning must be repeatable</h2><p>Partners need a consistent way to associate devices with the right customer, site, configuration, application and connectivity settings.</p><h2>Visibility reduces support cost</h2><p>Fleet status, version, last communication, configuration and diagnostic information can help support teams separate a local issue from a systemic one.</p><h2>Updates need governance</h2><p>Application and OS updates should be staged, approved, observable and recoverable rather than pushed without control.</p><h2>Channel structure matters</h2><p>Partners with resellers or multiple support tiers may need role-based visibility and customer segmentation rather than one flat device list.</p><div class="field-note"><h2>The customer does not separate your software from “their clock problem”</h2><p>When a clock is offline or out of date, the end customer usually sees one solution, even if several vendors are involved. A software partner therefore needs a clear way to know device state, route support and understand who owns the next action.</p><p>That operating visibility protects the partner’s customer experience. It also keeps device issues from consuming engineering teams that were hired to build workforce software, not troubleshoot field hardware one clock at a time.</p></div><div class="overlook"><div class="kicker">WHAT MOST BUYERS OVERLOOK</div><h2>Fleet state is a customer-experience problem.</h2><p>A software partner may think of clocks as customer-owned hardware. Customers often see them as part of the partner solution. If a device is misconfigured, outdated or unreachable, the support experience reflects on the software brand regardless of who technically owns the hardware.</p><p>Centralized operations should therefore make device identity, assignment, configuration, version, connectivity and support context visible enough to act on.</p></div><h2>Options and when to use each approach</h2><p class="options-note">Use the alternatives below to test fit, ownership and supportability in your environment. Start by asking: Who creates and assigns a new device?</p><div class="decision-grid"><div class="decision-card"><h3>Partner manages devices directly</h3><div class="when">When it fits</div><p>Partner builds/operates provisioning, configuration and support tooling.</p><div class="when">What to watch</div><p class="watch">Maximum control but substantial engineering and operations burden.</p></div><div class="decision-card"><h3>Vendor-managed device platform</h3><div class="when">When it fits</div><p>Use manufacturer cloud/device services for fleet operations.</p><div class="when">What to watch</div><p class="watch">Reduces partner burden; integration and branding/tenant requirements must fit.</p></div><div class="decision-card"><h3>Shared responsibility</h3><div class="when">When it fits</div><p>Vendor manages low-level device operations while partner manages customer-facing workflow and business configuration.</p><div class="when">What to watch</div><p class="watch">Often practical for software companies that want to own the customer experience.</p></div><div class="decision-card"><h3>Reseller/channel hierarchy</h3><div class="when">When it fits</div><p>Support partner → reseller → customer segmentation where required.</p><div class="when">What to watch</div><p class="watch">Important when multiple organizations need scoped visibility and control.</p></div></div><div class="framework"><h3>A practical decision framework</h3><ul><li>Make the decision around the workforce and data outcome: For software partners, device management is part of product quality because the customer experiences the hardware and software as one solution.</li><li>Resolve this design question early: Who can see logs and execute remote actions?</li><li>Assign ownership for exceptions, support and change before rollout.</li></ul></div><div class="mistakes"><h3>Common design mistakes</h3><ul><li>Making every reseller share one administrative view.</li><li>Separating device support data from the partner’s support workflow.</li><li>Failing to define who owns updates, configuration and hardware replacement.</li></ul></div><div class="proof-block"><div class="kicker">PUT THE DESIGN TO THE TEST</div><h2>A device-management platform should answer these questions quickly</h2><ul><li>Which customer, site and configuration owns this device?</li><li>When did it last communicate?</li><li>Which application and approved configuration are installed?</li><li>Which devices missed an update and why?</li><li>Can support retrieve useful diagnostics before deciding to replace hardware?</li></ul></div><div class="proof-block"><div class="kicker">PRACTICAL USE CASES</div><h2>Use cases that expose weak device operations</h2><ul><li>A new customer needs 80 devices provisioned with the same approved baseline.</li><li>A replacement device must inherit the exact site configuration without manual reconstruction.</li><li>A security or application release must be staged across representative customers before broad rollout.</li><li>A support team needs evidence before deciding whether to dispatch hardware.</li></ul><p>These are normal lifecycle events, not edge cases. If the operating model depends on spreadsheets, shared credentials or local memory, the fleet-management design is not ready for channel or enterprise scale.</p></div><div class="questions"><h2>Questions leaders should ask</h2><ul><li>Who creates and assigns a new device?</li><li>Who can see logs and execute remote actions?</li><li>How are customer/reseller boundaries enforced?</li><li>Who owns software-update scheduling?</li><li>What information does the partner support team need in its own UI?</li></ul></div><div class="perspective"><div class="label">ZKTeco WFM perspective</div><h2>Manage the fleet as part of the partner product—not as a collection of serial numbers.</h2><p>ZKTeco WFM treats device operations as a core part of software-partner enablement. CirrusConnect is the partner-oriented cloud/device layer, while Ultima provides a consistent endpoint family beneath it. The objective is to give partners a repeatable way to connect, organize and operate devices across customers and sites rather than recreate fleet tooling for every deployment.</p><p>That operating layer is especially important when a partner uses resellers or supports multiple customer environments. The system should preserve clear tenancy and ownership while giving the appropriate support team enough visibility to diagnose problems. Device management becomes valuable when it reduces uncertainty, truck rolls and support handoffs—not merely when it shows a green online indicator.</p></div><div class="takeaway"><h2>Key takeaway</h2><p>At enterprise scale, the time clock becomes a fleet and fleet state becomes part of the product experience. Centralize identity, configuration, versions, connectivity and diagnostics so support teams can act with evidence. For software partners, good device management protects both operating cost and the reputation of the WFM platform.</p></div><div class="disclaimer"><strong>Important information and disclaimer.</strong> This article is provided for general informational and educational purposes only. It is not legal, tax, HR, payroll, labor, regulatory, compliance, security, privacy, accounting, employment or policy advice. Organizations should consult qualified advisors regarding their specific requirements. Examples of workflows and capabilities are illustrative and may vary by product, configuration, integration, software platform and release. ZKTeco WFM evaluates customer and software-partner requirements and can recommend appropriate supported configurations, integrations, product capabilities, enhancements or customer-specific approaches where appropriate. Product specifications and capabilities are subject to change. Third-party names and trademarks belong to their respective owners.</div><div class="article-cta"><div class="cat">MAKE DEVICE OPERATIONS PART OF THE PRODUCT</div><h2>Offering Time Clocks Without Wanting to Build Device Management?</h2><p>Talk with ZKTeco WFM about centralized clock operations, support workflows and how CirrusConnect can fit your partner architecture.</p><a class="btn" href="../contact">Talk to an Expert</a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-box"><h4>Related insights</h4><a href="software-partner-build-vs-buy">Build vs. Buy: Should a WFM Software Company Build Its Own Time-Clock Stack?</a><a href="software-partner-expectations">What a WFM Software Company Should Expect From a Time-Clock Technology Partner</a><a href="sdk-app-middleware-choice">SDK vs. Prebuilt App vs. Cloud Middleware: Choosing the Right Time-Clock Integration Model</a><a href="apis-not-device-strategy">Open APIs Are Important. They Are Not a Complete Time-Clock Strategy.</a></div><div class="side-box"><h4>About these articles</h4><div class="side-note">ZKTeco WFM Insights are educational perspectives for enterprise HR, payroll, IT, operations, WFM and software leaders. They are not legal or policy advice.</div></div><div class="side-box"><h4>Explore</h4><a href="../software-partners">Software partner technology</a><a href="../ultima-series">Ultima Series</a><a href="../timetrack">TimeTrack</a><a href="../cirrusconnect">CirrusConnect</a></div></aside></main><footer class="zk-global-footer"><div class="zk-footer-shell"><div class="zk-footer-grid"><div class="zk-footer-brand"><img alt="ZKTeco WFM" class="zk-footer-logo" src="/assets/ZKTecowfm-white-green@4x.png" width="180"/><p>The Workforce Data Collection Company.<br/>Every Punch Matters.</p></div><div class="zk-footer-col"><h4>Solutions</h4><a href="../workday">Workday Customers</a><a href="../software-partners">Software Partners</a><a href="../use-cases">Industry Use Cases</a><a href="../customer-stories">Customer Stories</a></div><div class="zk-footer-col"><h4>Products</h4><a href="../ultima-series">Ultima Series</a><a href="../timetrack">TimeTrack</a><a href="../cirrusconnect">CirrusConnect</a><a href="../workday#cirrusdcs">CirrusDCS</a></div><div class="zk-footer-col"><h4>Resources &amp; Trust</h4><a